[lib/internal/webstreams/adapters.js] The `destroy()` is needed here. Consider this simplified version of what the WPT `compression-bad-chunks.any.js` tests do:

```js
const cs = new CompressionStream('deflate');
const reader = cs.readable.getReader();
const writer = cs.writable.getWriter();
const writePromise = writer.write(3.14); // bad chunk, zlib throws synchronously
const readPromise = reader.read();
await writePromise; // rejects ✅ (with or without destroy)
await readPromise;  // WITHOUT destroy: hangs forever ❌
```

Per the spec, `CompressionStream` and `DecompressionStream` are `TransformStream`s. The `destroy()` replicates the `TransformStream` semantics: error both sides on any throw in the transform path.

