human_review:
[lib/internal/fs/promises.js] I believe the convention is to make these explicitly wrapped/awaited, for stacktracing etc. (#58400 et al.)

```suggestion
  const remove = async () => await lazyRimRaf()(fullPath, {
    maxRetries: 0,
    recursive: true,
    retryDelay: 0,
  });
```

[lib/internal/fs/promises.js] ```suggestion
    remove,
    async [SymbolAsyncDispose]() {
      await remove();
    },
```

[doc/api/fs.md] What about the callback-based version of this?

```js
fs.mkdirTempDisposable('prefix', (disposer) => {
  ??
});
```

I'm not convinced we should have it but we have sync/callback/promise based versions of all the methods so this would be a departure from the norm that should at least be discussed. I'm almost wondering if we shouldn't just have the one version of the method accessible via `fs.promises` and not worry about this sync function variation at all.

[lib/internal/fs/promises.js] For readability I recommend separating out the `lazyRimRaf` call into a separate line...

like..

```
const rmrf = lazyRimRaf();
const remove = async() => rmrf(...);
```

[lib/fs.js] In case `rmSync` throws, let's make it possible to tell from the stack trace if it happened in explicit call or due to `using`.
```suggestion
    [SymbolDispose]() {
      remove();
    },
```

[doc/contributing/ai-contributions.md] And on what basis does one determine this decision? Here, let me give you two examples... one of these snippets was written by me, the other by Claude: which one is acceptable and which one is not?

Option A:
```js
for (let n = 0; n < 1000; n++) {
  console.log('Am I AI generated?');
}
```

Option B:
```js
let n = 0;
while (n++ < 1000) {
  console.log('Or am I AI generated?');
}
```

What this part of the policy basically boils down to is: any contribution can be contested and our normal consensus seeking rules are to be followed to resolve the contention. That's no different than what we have now except for the added incentive for a contributor to just lie about whether they used an agent to help write the code. If the policy is unenforceable it's not useful.

